Model, with no capability work at all, the one thing the discipline forbids: joining free runs.

WHY ASK THIS SEPARATELY. Sublet merges two regions through a handle taken BEFORE the split that
separated them, and revoking that handle kills everything below it. MicroPython carves from the
front of a free region, so the splits form a right-leaning chain and the common ancestor of two
adjacent free regions always covers everything between and to the right of them, live objects
included. There is therefore no handle that merges exactly two neighbours, and free space is
reusable only inside the region it was carved from.

That is a property of the allocator's shape, not of the capability machinery, so it can be
measured without any: one bit a block saying a region begins here, and a search loop that restarts
its run at a region start instead of continuing through it. The model is exact because the carve
is always from a region's base, which makes a region either wholly free or wholly one object, so a
usable free run always begins at a region start.

WHAT IT COSTS, MEASURED OVER THE UPSTREAM SUITE AT THE FULL FEATURE LEVEL:

                          joining        not joining
  suite            551 pass, 1 fail   547 pass, 5 fail
  collections                   53                475      nine times as many
  collected per cycle         4728                541
  allocations              369,904            363,136      6,768 of them failed

The four tests that break are dict_del.py, memoryview_gc.py, set_binop.py and set_remove.py, and
each dies with MemoryError: memory allocation failed, at 736 and 368 bytes. That is fragmentation
and nothing else.

SIBLING MERGING DOES NOT RESCUE IT, which is worth writing down because it is the obvious next
idea. Carving from the front makes R0 into O1 and R1, then R1 into O2 and R2. By the time O1 dies
its sibling R1 has been split again, and the handle that covers them both covers O2 as well. The
same holds for every pair. Imposing a mergeable tree would mean changing the fit strategy, which
is a policy change, and the arm this is measured against is the same allocator with the same
policy.

So this is not a step toward the port. It is the measurement that says what the port would cost,
and it is the reason to spend the next week on an allocator whose freed space returns through a
structure rather than into a flat bitmap.

